   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A function \\( f \\) has derivatives of all orders. The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = 0 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\). It is known that \\( f^{(5)}(0) = 4 \\) and that \\( |f^{(5)}(x)| \\le 10 \\) for all \\( x \\) between \\( 0 \\) and \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\). The approximation is meant to be within \\( \\frac{1}{100} \\) of \\( f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         384
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 4 polynomial needs the maximum of \\( |f^{(5)}(x)| \\), the derivative of order \\( 5 \\), between the centre and the input.",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - 0|^{5}}{5!} \\) with \\( M = 10 \\) and \\( |x - 0| = \\frac{1}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            384
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluating gives \\( \\frac{10 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{5}}{120} = \\frac{1}{384} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            384
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{1}{384} \\), which is at most \\( \\frac{1}{100} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            384
         ]
      }
   ],
